\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Which inflow integers agree under refinement}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Use the same oriented axes, spin-fiber and grading on the two grids, with common dimensionless mass #{\mu=aM=a'M'}. For periods at least #{3} the free Wilson \vocabk{bulk integer}{fdip-019W} is unchanged whenever the number of even periods is unchanged. It is therefore unchanged under every odd refinement factor and under every refinement of an all-even grid. For the signed bottom-face dimension of \ref{fdip-01AI} at #{\mu=2j+1}, with the same face, trivial transverse links there and the slab hypotheses of \ref{fdip-01AH}, the criterion is equality of #{\binom{e_T}{j}} on the two grids, where #{e_T} is the number of even transverse periods and the spin-fiber is nonzero.}

\p{In two dimensions on even periods at least #{4}, the scalar-field refinement of \ref{fdip-01BW} preserves the overlap index whenever the coarse links and mass obey \ref{fdip-019X}.}

\proof{\p{By \ref{fdip-019Y} the vertex formula is #{\sum_{0\le j\le e,\,2j<\mu}(-1)^j\binom{e}{j}} for #{e} even periods. In the interpolated count, \ref{fdip-01BX} instead includes each subset #{J} with #{\sum_{j\in J}b_j<\mu}. Evaluate each sum with the coarse periods and again with the fine periods to determine its change. For each odd axis #{b_j} increases with its refined period until it reaches #{2} on an even grid. Thus a summand can drop only if its coarse threshold is below #{\mu} and its fine threshold is at least #{\mu}. The exact failures occur already in \ref{fdip-01BY}.}

\p{For the slab, \ref{fdip-01AI} gives the bottom signed dimension #{(\dim S)(\dim F)(-1)^j\binom{e_T}{j}/2}. With one transverse period #{3} refined to #{6} and #{j=1}, the two binomial values are #{0} and #{1}, because the transverse #{\pi} label first occurs on the fine grid. Finally the scalar flux is unchanged by \ref{fdip-01BW}, and each fine angle has magnitude at most the old maximum divided by #{k^2}. Thus the same explicit admissibility bound of \ref{fdip-019X} holds. Its other two factors, the signed multiplicity and the all-even bulk integer, are unchanged. Its mass-dependent indices on the two grids agree.}}
